gram but also  is going to let us really work in those  kind of interesting experiences with  working with industry working with  government and working with people in  the medical industry  as well so i just want to put in a plug  for this we are  you know recruiting for our next cohort  starting next year anybody is interested  in our program uh please feel free to to  reach out to us at the bioinformatics  uw.edu email address  so without further ado on to the  research talk that i want to give for  today  um  so  my entire career has been you know based  around looking at microbes and what they  do  um and i think you know we've seen in  the past you know decade or so that  the the the role of microbes the  microbiome associated with humans and  other organisms they really started to  take a much more central role than they  ever had in the past  so i i if we look at this kind of extent  of genetic diversity on earth  and we look at this tree here you can  see that  this little red circle at the bottom  pretty much every organism that you can  see with your eye is within that red  circle all the rest of this diversity  it's microorganisms from bacteria to  archaea to microbial eukaryotes so it's  really is a you know it's a it's a  microbial world i don't think this is  any secret uh but even this uh tree does  not capture that full extent there's  obviously one missing piece what is it  um well it's viruses right  so  viruses obviously have been very much in  our consciousness than the last couple  of years with uh the covet epidemic  um but i tend to to research more on a  different group of viruses  which are the the microbial viruses  which would be any virus that infects  one of those microorganisms that i  mentioned  uh and these are you know by far the  most diverse group of biological  entities on the planet the the  if there is a gene on this planet  probably a microbial virus is carrying  around some version of that gene  um and they are probably one of the most  poorly or least poorly understood groups  of or of you know biological entities i  always hasten to call them  organisms  on the planet and  that's a lot of what my research has  been based around is how to figure out  ways to understand viruses and how they  impact their environment  so  i i have a joint research group here at  the university of delaware i've actually  teamed up with two other faculty members  eric womack and jeff furman and we've  you know developed a joint group that  looks at different aspects of these  virus microbe  environment interactions in that  environment really it could be anything  um you know i've looked at this is just  a small selection of things i have  looked at  but it could be anything from you see  seagrass here coral seawater  but also humans right humans are an  environment and a major environment for  microbes so looking at humans and  biomedical models agricultural models  you can see soybean here  these are the types of projects we do  but what unifies them is this interest  in the microorganisms  how those microorganisms interact with  these either environments or are  eukaryotic organisms  and then ultimately how the viruses that  infect those microbes uh play into that  so it's kind of a tripartite system  we're looking at  and to to study this you know obviously  dna you know those types of omics  techniques are the bread and butter of  looking at these types of systems uh so  those are really kind of the unifying  themes that go through my research  so  you know just as a basic background  viruses are really difficult to study if  you can go out and isolate a virus and  sequence this genome you know you're  golden you can do a lot of research with  it but it is very very hard to do that  even when we know what the host is say  human it is often very difficult to go  out and find specific viruses that are  infecting that host much less if you're  looking at environmental samples where  there could be thousands of potential  hosts and thousands or tens of thousands  of different types of viruses and  figuring out which virus goes with which  host and what can you host can you  culture the host can you culture the  virus  so there's a there's a lot of  complexity in trying to study viruses  so dna-based methods have really become  important for looking at viruses however  a lot of the tools that allow us to use  uh omix-based techniques and other  organisms like marker genes that are  universal say the 16s rna gene that gets  used in bacteria don't exist in viruses  there is no one gene that every virus  has  so  it makes it quite difficult quite quite  complex to look at  so using shotgun metagenomics which you  know would be going out into an  environment taking a sample that has a  broad mixture of organisms in it  typically what we'll do is we will  filter out the cellular organisms into a  separate fraction so that what's left is  just things of a size that should be  viruses  and then we sequence that entire sample  right so we get a huge amount of dna  sequencing data back and it's about many  many many different types of viruses  that might be living in an individual  sample  so then we use various techniques to go  through and look at that and some of  those techniques can be quite  computationally expensive uh but you  know as computing and other technologies  have advanced that's really not a  limiting factor in doing these types of  of techniques at this point at this  point really our limiting factor is our  ability to make biological  interpretations out of those genomic  data that we get i'd say in viruses it's  really hard because as i mentioned you  can't culture many of them so for us to  actually have phenotypes in the viruses  that we could potentially correlate with  things we're seeing genotypically in  that metagenomic data it becomes a real  challenge for us  so  you know  other other issues that we run into are  things like if we look at any given  environment and we metagenomically  sequence it and then we cluster all the  putative proteins that are there  and we look at them  from the most common to the least common  among the most common proteins one study  we have here we found that 62 of the 100  most abundant putative proteins were  uncharacterized  and of the ones which is saying they  were not in a genome at that point  and of the ones that were actually in a  genome all the ones that have arrows  pointing to them here were you know they  were annotated as puted of  uncharacterized protein which might as  well be  uncharacterized uh or are unknown uh for  for all intensive purposes so this is a  major wall we also run into is that you  know there's a lot of genomic data out  there but we don't even know what gene  it corresponds to so much less what type  of activity it may have  so it's been an ongoing challenge and  probably one of the things that keeps me  interested in this work uh is that it's  challenging to go through these data and  figure out ways to make meaningful  biological conclusions based on them  uh so i mentioned you know undergraduate  researchers have played a huge role in  shaping this research program  and this is a this is actually a slide  from about 11 years ago this is a  program that we've had going on for  quite a while  but what we started to do  with my collaborators was to  each summer recruit a cohort of  undergraduate researchers into the lab  and we're lucky to have excellent summer  undergraduate research programs at the  university of delaware that have helped  us to do this and to bring these  students in  and we really put them to work on doing  metagenomic analysis on doing some of  the lab-based steps downstream of it  so that we can start to understand a  little bit more about this diversity  that is out there  so one of the main approaches we've used  with them is we will you know we'll take  these protein clusters that we see in  metagenomes we'll have them explore them  we'll have them  adopt a particular cluster or a  particular type of cluster i have  ribonucleotide reductase clusters kind  of pointed out in this graph here  so  one undergraduate researcher may take  these and they will they will actually  go through the metagenomic data pull out  the sequence reads for those uh those  that contain those genes  do assemblies  do phylogenetic analyses ultimately  build some primers so that they could  look at this and try to actually  identify it in environmental samples and  then you know take some environmental  samples that we have in our library and  see if they can actually re-identify  these genes in those  natural samples and through doing this  it really increased our bandwidth to be  able to look at all that metagenomic  data that we were holding on to and  really start to understand some of these  genes that we see very commonly in uh  the the various metagenome sequences and  you know i i we've definitely looked at  both unknown and known uh functional  genes through these types of methods and  we have interesting stories on both  today i'm going to follow through with  one story that is from a known gene  which would be dna polymerase one  so if you're not familiar it is the  proofreading polymerase of many bacteria  but in  viruses that carry out it it is the main  polymerase it is the one they use for  replicating their genome  and it is a fairly common gene for a  double-stranded dna virus to carry we  see it in about 25  of known dna viruses double-stranded dna  viruses so that's actually a pretty good  percentage for us so looking at this  gene gives us the ability to look across  a lot of different viruses  so  one group that has looked at this and  there are a couple of grad students just  chopic and dan nasco  also eric sakowski who's not on here and  in particular one undergrad helen  schmidt  who really adopted this dna polymerase  gene over the course of several years  and made some really interesting  discoveries with it that i wanted to  walk you through  so  uh first is a little bit of background  to remind you  viruses and microbial viruses in  particular tend to come in two  lifestyles or flavors  um there are the viruses that will enter  a cell and immediately they're going to  go into the replicative cycle make a lot  of copies of themselves and immediately  lyse the cell so those are the ones that  are virulent or litholite the lytic  lifestyle by default  the other group are the temperate phage  or temperate viruses those are the ones  that enter the cell incorporate  themselves into the host genome  will lie dormant for some period of time  and then eventually some trigger in the  cell or the cell's environment will  cause it to essentially activate itself  and then do a replication so  understanding which viruses are lytic  and lysogenic  is a pretty pretty important thing when  you're trying to understand viral  biology and how viruses impact their  host  so  it seems like this would be a very easy  thing to identify within a sequence but  it's not always and actually many many  many phage that are out there we have  complete genome sequences but we still  don't know for sure which one of these  lifestyles  those phage tend to follow  so  helen schmidt as i mentioned she was a  undergraduate biochemistry major  and she came into one of our summer  undergraduate internship  opportunities and adopted this dna  polymerase one  cluster and started looking at it in  much more detail  and through her work and her particular  interest in biochemistry  she noticed something within this gene  that  has led to 12 years of subsequent  research for us  and that was the fact that within this  gene when you look at the the protein  sequence for it there is an individual  amino acid residue within it that seems  to be predictive for the phage lifestyle  if we look at this  residue at position 762 relative to the  e coli version of the gene  the the default