e going to get a mutant and maybe  that mutant is actually a good thing  right maybe that's new diversity that's  going to allow them to succeed but if  that mutant actually is bad then you  know  it it's okay you've got lots of copies  that are faithful to the original  um  you know and occasionally you're gonna  get a really crazy mutation which uh you  know may may or may not be a good thing  uh but you have to see how that works  out with natural selection but the  really interesting thing with this trait  is you're you're modifying your  polymerase and your polymerase is  ultimately responsible for the mutations  that could lead to natural selection  lead to evolution so really by modifying  their polymerase in this way or you know  naturally selecting for the polymerase  in this way they're evolving to increase  the rate of of evolution which you know  that's a phenomenal thought right  they're they're they're really giving  themselves the ability to be more  diverse  if we look at the other side of the of  the coin temperate phage tend to  replicate slower they're sitting within  their cell for a long time so they  really need to be more in sync with the  cell and then when they actually do  start to replicate themselves they're  doing it at a critical time when there's  probably a stress on the cell and they  really need to get accurate copies and  get out so that they can survive and  reinfect a new cell so  in those phage you know selecting for a  more accurate mutation this leucine  mutation even though it's a bit slower  may have significant evolutionary uh of  potential for you know keeping it in  sync with its host  so for the rest of my talk uh actually  one more slide on this so you know the  the first students i talked about they  were doing that process that whole  process very manually we've actually  since done a lot of work on streamlining  it making it easier building  bioinformatics tools i've had a number  of grad students who have come through  and we've just made an entire suite of  bioinformatics tools that make this  process of investigating these viral  genes in this way uh very  straightforward  and easy to do  so  for the rest of the talk if we go back  to this dna polymerase a you know we  we've looked at plenty of other clusters  but this one has really been one that  has really captured our our interest  uh and i i mentioned this this thousand  times faster but not quite as accurate  or three times more accurate and a  thousand times slower  those are really you know fairly  widespread in biochemical literature but  when we really traced it back they all  went back to single studies where they  tried some in vitro  mutations so really we didn't know for  sure that all polymerases followed this  rule or that it's something we would  actually see in the environment  so a current study we have ongoing now  is actually taking the bioinformatics  back into the lab  and i have a student rachel keown  who's  been working with some collaborators at  virgin biosciences and what we've done  is actually taken our metagenomic  sequences for dna polymerase a  and have picked out different variants  of them across the tree represent these  different types of amino acid residues  that come from different sources and  we've had them synthesized had the dna  synthesized and then cloned those into  expression vectors so that we can  actually produce the protein and do  biochemical characterizations of it  so  this was something a little bit out of  our wheelhouse and we were really hoping  it was going to work and fortunately you  know working with these experts at  virgin  it's working we have actually taken  we've had about 50 of these synthesized  at this point and we've had about a 60  success rate of being able to isolate a  protein out of it and that protein  actually having functionality  so  you know it's really cool that we can  actually take a sequence from a  metagenome and make a protein out of it  