nds of potential  hosts and thousands or tens of thousands  of different types of viruses and  figuring out which virus goes with which  host and what can you host can you  culture the host can you culture the  virus  so there's a there's a lot of  complexity in trying to study viruses  so dna-based methods have really become  important for looking at viruses however  a lot of the tools that allow us to use  uh omix-based techniques and other  organisms like marker genes that are  universal say the 16s rna gene that gets  used in bacteria don't exist in viruses  there is no one gene that every virus  has  so  it makes it quite difficult quite quite  complex to look at  so using shotgun metagenomics which you  know would be going out into an  environment taking a sample that has a  broad mixture of organisms in it  typically what we'll do is we will  filter out the cellular organisms into a  separate fraction so that what's left is  just things of a size that should be  viruses  and then we sequence that entire sample  right so we get a huge amount of dna  sequencing data back and it's about many  many many different types of viruses  that might be living in an individual  sample  so then we use various techniques to go  through and look at that and some of  those techniques can be quite  computationally expensive uh but you  know as computing and other technologies  have advanced that's really not a  limiting factor in doing these types of  of techniques at this point at this  point really our limiting factor is our  ability to make biological  interpretations out of those genomic  data that we get i'd say in viruses it's  really hard because as i mentioned you  can't culture many of them so for us to  actually have phenotypes in the viruses  that we could potentially correlate with  things we're seeing genotypically in  that metagenomic data it becomes a real  challenge for us  so  you know  other other issues that we run into are  things like if we look at any given  environment and we metagenomically  sequence it and then we cluster all the  putative proteins that are there  and we look at them  from the most common to the least common  among the most common proteins one study  we have here we found that 62 of the 100  most abundant putative proteins were  uncharacterized  and of the ones which is saying they  were not in a genome at that point  and of the ones that were actually in a  genome all the ones that have arrows  pointing to them here were you know they  were annotated as puted of  uncharacterized protein which might as  well be  uncharacterized uh or are unknown uh for  for all intensive purposes so this is a  major wall we also run into is that you  know there's a lot of genomic data out  there but we don't even know what gene  it corresponds to so much less what type  of activity it may have  so it's been an ongoing challenge and  probably one of the things that keeps me  interested in this work uh is that it's  challenging to go through these data and  figure out ways to make meaningful  biological conclusions based on them  uh so i mentioned you know undergraduate  researchers have played a huge role in  shaping this research program  and this is a this is actually a slide  from about 11 years ago this is a  program that we've had going on for  quite a while  but what we started to do  with my collaborators was to  each summer recruit a cohort of  undergraduate researchers into the lab  and we're lucky to have excellent summer  undergraduate research programs at the  university of delaware that have helped  us to do this and to bring these  students in  and we really put them to work on doing  metagenomic analysis on doing some of  the lab-based steps downstream of it  so that we can start to understand a  little bit more about this diversity  that is out there  so one of the main approaches we've used  with them is we will you know we'll take  these protein clusters that we see in  metagenomes we'll have them explore them  we'll have them  adopt a particular cluster or a  particular type of cluster i have  ribonucleotide reductase clusters kind  of pointed out in this graph here  so  one undergraduate researcher may take  these and they will they will actually  go through the metagenomic data pull out  the sequence reads for those uh those  that contain those genes  do assemblies  do phylogenetic analyses ultimately  build some primers so that they could  look at this and try to actually  identify it in environmental samples and  then you know take some environmental  samples that we have in our library and  see if they can actually re-identify  these genes in those  natural samples and through doing this  it really increased our bandwidth to be  able to look at all that metagenomic  data that we were holding on to and  really start to understand some of these  genes that we see very commonly in uh  the the various metagenome sequences and  you know i i we've definitely looked at  both unknown and known uh functional  genes through these types of methods and  we have interesting stories on both  today i'm going to follow through with  one story that is from a known gene  which would be dna polymerase one  so if you're not familiar it is the  proofreading polymerase of many bacteria  but in  viruses that carry out it it is the main  polymerase it is the one they use for  replicating their genome  and it is a fairly common gene for a  double-stranded dna virus to carry we  see it in about 25  of known dna viruses double-stranded dna  viruses so that's actually a pretty good  percentage for us so looking at this  gene gives us the ability to look across  a lot of different viruses  so  one group that has looked at this and  there are a couple of grad students just  chopic and dan nasco  also eric sakowski who's not on here and  in particular one undergrad helen  schmidt  who really adopted this dna polymerase  gene over the course of several years  and made some really interesting  discoveries with it that i wanted to  walk you through  so  uh first is a little bit of background  to remind you  viruses and microbial viruses in  particular tend to come in two  lifestyles or flavors  um there are the viruses that will enter  a cell and immediately they're going to  go into the replicative cycle make a lot  of copies of themselves and immediately  lyse the cell so those are the ones that  are virulent or litholite the lytic  lifestyle by default  the other group are the temperate phage  or temperate viruses those are the ones  that enter the cell incorporate  themselves into the host genome  will lie dormant for some period of time  and then eventually some trigger in the  cell or the cell's environment will  cause it to essentially activate itself  and then do a replication so  understanding which viruses are lytic  and lysogenic  is a pretty pretty important thing when  you're trying to understand viral  biology and how viruses impact their  host  so  it seems like this would be a very easy  thing to identify within a sequence but  it's not always and actually many many  many phage that are out there we have  complete genome sequences but we still  don't know for sure which one of these  lifestyles  those phage tend to follow  so  helen schmidt as i mentioned she was a  undergraduate biochemistry major  and she came into one of our summer  undergraduate internship  opportunities and adopted this dna  polymerase one  cluster and started looking at it in  much more detail  and through her work and her particular  interest in biochemistry  she noticed something within this gene  that  has led to 12 years of subsequent  research for us  and that was the fact that within this  gene when you look at the the protein  sequence for it there is an individual  amino acid residue within it that seems  to be predictive for the phage lifestyle  if we look at this  residue at position 762 relative to the  e coli version of the gene  the the default that you tend to see in  bacteria is a phenylalanine at that  position  however if tyrosine is at that position  there is biochemical data out there that  indicates that that makes the polymerase  about a thousand times more processive  faster  essentially but also makes it more prone  to errors while it's replicating  um there's also a mutation that's been  biochemically characterized to leucine  at that location and when that amino  acid is in there we get about three  times more accurate but about a thousand  times slower than the phenylalanine  so it's quite a spread in the speed and  the accuracy that occurs  based on this one amino acid change in  polymerases and what helen noticed was  that this individual amino acid residue  when she overlaid its identity onto a  tree  very neatly clades with our groups with  the clades that were on the tree we can  cleanly see there are two groups here  that are losing but there's a single  group each for phenylalanine and  tyrosine so to us that was an indication  wow there is a real  uh biological  phenomenon going on with this amino acid  residue  and we really saw that when she started  to look at the lifestyle of the phage  that we did know their um their  lifestyle and that was that the the  tyrosine version was always lytic we had  no exceptions at the time of a lysogenic  uh phage that had tyrosine phenylalanine  was almost always lytic a few exceptions  there  and leucine was almost always lysogenic  so just this individual residue gave us  that information that otherwise is very  very difficult for us to identify out of  these types of sequences  so if we just do the thought of  experiment of why this might be  important to viral biology  let's think about you know lydic viruses  are the ones that come into their host  they replicate fast they produce lots of  copies of themselves and then they're  out of the cell  so for those guys if they have errors in  a few copies  maybe that's okay maybe occasionally  you're going to get a mutant and maybe  that mutant is actually a good thing  right maybe that's new diversity that's  going to allow them to succeed but if  that mutant actually is bad then you  know  it it's okay you've got lots of copies  that are faithful to the original  um  you know and occasionally you're gonna  get a really crazy mutation which uh you  know may may or may not be a good thing  uh but you have to see how that works  out with natural selection but the  really interesting thing with this trait  is you're you're modifying your  polymerase and your polymerase is  ultimately responsible for the mutations  that could lead to natural selection  lead to evolution so really by modifying  their polymerase in this way or you know  naturally selecting for the polymerase  in this way they're evolving to increase  the rate of of evolution which you know  that's a phenomenal thought right  they're they're they're really giving  themselves the ability to be more  diverse  if we look at the other side of the of  the coin temperate phage tend to  replicate slower they're sitting within  their cell for a long time so they  really need to be more in sync with the  cell and then when they actually do  start to replicate themselves they're  doing it at a critical time when there's  probably a stress on the cell and they  really need to get accurate copies and  get out so that they can survive and  reinfect a new cell so  in those phage you know selecting for a  more accurate mutation this leucine  mutation even though it's a bit slower  may have significant evolutionary uh of  potential for you know keeping it in  sync with its host  so for the rest of my talk uh actually  one more slide on this so you know the  the first students i talked about they  were doing that process that whole  process very manually we've actually  since done a lot of work on streamlining  it making it easier building  bioinformatics tools i've had a number  of grad students who have come through  and we've just made an entire suite of  bioinformatics tools that make this  process of investigating these viral  genes in this way uh very  straightforward  and easy to do  so  for the rest of the talk if we go back  to this dna polymerase a you know we  we've looked at plenty of other clusters  but this one has really been one that  has really captured our our interest  uh and i i mentioned this this thousand  times faster but not quite as accurate  or three times more accurate and a  thousand times slower  those are really you know fairly  widespread in biochemical literature but  when we really traced it back they all  went back to single studies where they  tried some in vitro  mutations so really we didn't know for  sure that all polymerases followed this  rule or that it's something we would  actually see in the environment  so a current study we have ongoing now  is actually taking the bioinformatics  back into the lab  and i have a student rachel keown  who's  been working with some collaborators at  virgin biosciences and what we've done  is actually taken our metagenomic  sequences for dna polymerase a  and have picked out different variants  of them across the tree represent these  different types of amino acid residues  that come from different sources and  we've had them synthesized had the dna  synthesized and then cloned those into  expression vectors so that we can  actually produce the protein and do  biochemical characterizations of it  so  this was something a little bit out of  our wheelhouse and we were really hoping  it was going to work and fortunately you  know working with these experts at  virgin  it's working we have actually taken  we've had about 50 of these synthesized  at this point and we've had about a 60  success rate of being able to isolate a  protein out of it and that protein  actually having functionality  so  you know it's really cool that we can  actually take a sequence from a  metagenome and make a protein out of it  but what we're ultimately interested in  is we want to find viral phenotypes and  the biochemistry of these enzymes  ultimately determine how fast that virus  can replicate how fast it can copy  itself and get out of its host cell  so understanding the different  phenotypes biochemically for this enzyme  is something really important that we  can now layer on to other data that we  have so  you know i i won't go into explaining  this figure because i'm getting a little  short on time but the the bottom line is  if we look through uh these different  ones that we have synthesized we have  found quite a few of them that we can  show have primer extension activity at  various speeds to various rates  we also have several of them that have a  strand displacement activity which is an  interesting activity uh on its own um  that our by our our partners in industry  are very interested in but we also are  interested in is that it's another  phenotype that that organism has or that  virus has that it's carrying around that  we can potentially look at  so rachel is moving forward with this  work  she's gone a little bit beyond only  looking at this biochemical data that  she's been producing but she's also  looked more at  what else goes with the polymerase it's  not living in a vacuum it's ultimately  working with other genes to replicate  the genome  and we really see that there are some  really constant associations between our  different tyrosine pulley phenylalanine  pulley leucine pulley with different  types of helicase genes that are  specifically tuned to work with  individual ones so this is another piece  of evidence that this processivity  accuracy  hypothesis that we've been testing may  well be true in that  gp4 helicases for instance are known to  be among the fastest helicases out there  and if you're unwinding the dna for a  very fast polymerase you would think  you're going to need a very fast  helicase  so that we're seeing these associations  is very promising and we're now starting  to move beyond the pole a and also look  at the biochemistry of these accessory  genes like helicases like ribonucleotide  reductases  that we see in association with these  and look at that broader genome net  neighborhood and figure out how that  module of genome actually impacts the  biology of the phage  so where are we going with this work um  you know ultimately rachel's still  working on purifying and biochemically  characterizing those various enzymes  she's worked with another group of  undergraduates this summer in doing  optimal ph and temperature assays  which can actually tell us a little bit  about the biology of the cells that the  phage are infecting based on where their  optimal amplification or where their  optimal operational temperatures and phs  may fall  as we're expanding this database of  biochemical information about different  phage from different phylogenetic parts  of the tree  we're correlating that data with other  information that we're we either have  based on where they were isolated or  abundance and infectivity information  that we're we're producing in other  areas of our lab  through various collaborations and our  ultimate goal here is we're going to  take this massive data both on  biochemistry observable phenotypes when  we can get them as well as the genotypic  features that seem to correlate with  these and we're going to integrate them  together and begin to  see if we can make models that will  allow us to predict phenotype more  accurately based on the genotypic  features that we've seen within these  replication module proteins  and how well it can interpret or  predict these types of infectivity  characteristics or environmental  characteristics  that may apply and we've done this on  some fairly simple basis so far just  looking at combinations of  ribonucleotide reductases and dna  polymerases that we see occurring  together and we've actually had some  really interesting  results come out of that where we can  see correlations between  versions of ribonucleotide reductase  that require oxygen versus don't require  oxygen and the the available oxygen or  metal cofactors  that are available in their system  so we have some great preliminary data  here and we're really hoping to build a  more  substantial model moving forward  with our work  so i think i am wrapped up now um thanks  to everyone in my lab and my  collaborators and of course our funding  sources  thank you dr paulson that is again  fascinating stuff if you don't mind you  can quit sharing your screen so we can  see you  how are these in silico tools like alpha  fold for research you're working on how  useful are they  they're actually quite useful i actually  have a grad student uh zach schreiber  and a collaborator marty belcade at the  university of hawaii and they are  looking at particularly at protein  structure uh that we can predict with  these new and silico alpha fold two and  so forth uh and how well that can be  used to classify these proteins and  metagenomes so that's that's actually a  different direction we're going but it  is something we are investigating in  this area  very fascinating how important is  mentorship and student participation in  this research you've mentioned a whole  bunch of names that aren't yours during  this  yeah absolutely so you know student  participation is you know our research  could not happen without it both  undergrads and grads and we really try  to set up a kind of a a group support  mechanism we don't just take an  individual student through we bring  through a cohort of students which  brings a support mechanism with it in  itself and then we pair those students  those undergraduates with graduate  students and then we have mentor pi  meetings with them on a regular basis  so through that kind of multi-level  mentoring  it both teaches graduate students to be  mentors but it also sets up multiple  levels of support for those students as  they move forward  to to you know get get at these types of  new skills that they're trying to to  learn and apply  thank you so much  i've wrapped up our time with you and we  really appreciate that i think we could  ask questions for another half hour if  we could but we need to move on so thank  you again for your participation here  this afternoon we really appreciate it  thank you thank you
 

